1. What LoRA trains: W stays frozen, and the update is B x A, of rank r

  a 4,096 x 4,096 projection has 16,777,216 weights;
  B (4,096 x r) and A (r x 4,096) have 2 x 4,096 x r between them

   rank  trained parameters  share of model  adapter, 16-bit
      4           4,194,304          0.052%             8 MB
      8           8,388,608          0.105%            17 MB
     16          16,777,216          0.210%            34 MB
     64          67,108,864          0.839%           134 MB

2. Memory to train, before activations

  full fine-tune                    128.0 GB
  LoRA, rank 16, base in 16-bit      16.3 GB
  LoRA, rank 16, base in 4-bit        4.5 GB
  (the 4-bit row adds 6% for quantisation scales; activations come on top of all)

3. How much of an update a rank-r approximation keeps

  an update that is mostly low-rank  r=1: 14%  r=8: 90%  r=32: 92%  r=128: 96%
  an update with no structure        r=1: 1%  r=8: 6%  r=32: 21%  r=128: 62%

LoRA's bet is that the change fine-tuning needs looks like the first row: a few
directions carry nearly all of it. When the change does not — a new language, a
large body of new knowledge — a small rank is more likely to fall short, which is
one more reason facts belong in retrieval.
